+# Deploying Safex
+
+Everything below is a checklist you run from your own accounts. The agent sandbox has no
+outbound network (no GitHub, no Vercel, no Supabase), so none of these steps can be
+performed for you from here — but nothing in them is guesswork either. Each value is one
+the code actually reads, and the verification commands are the ones used in development.
+
+Order: **GitHub → Vercel → employee data → Supabase (optional) → security check**.
+
+---
+
+## 1. GitHub
+
+The work lives on the branch `arena/01a10d33-safexnew`. Commits made after a coding
+session closes stay on your machine until a new session pushes them.
+
+```bash
+git log --oneline -3            # confirm the commits are there
+git push origin arena/01a10d33-safexnew
+```
+
+If the push is refused because the session has ended, start a new coding session on this
+repository and push from there. Then open a pull request into `main` and merge it, so
+Vercel deploys from `main`.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Vercel
+
+1. <https://vercel.com/new> → **Import Git Repository** → pick `Musahid33/Safexnew`.
+2. Framework preset: **Next.js**. Build command, output directory and install command are
+   detected correctly — change nothing.
+3. Add the environment variables from section 3 **before** the first deploy. Set each one
+   for Production, Preview and Development.
+4. Deploy. Every later push to `main` redeploys automatically.
+
+**Cost, honestly.** The Hobby plan is free and stays free, but Vercel's terms restrict it
+to non-commercial use. A safety app run for a contracting company is commercial, so plan
+on Pro (currently USD 20 per user per month) rather than discovering the limit later.
+"Free for life" is true for a personal project and not true for this one.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Environment variables
+
+`.env.example` is the full list with comments. The ones that matter for a first deploy:
+
+| Variable | Value | Why |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `SAFEX_ADMIN_USERNAME` | your chosen user ID | One account covers Admin, HSE Manager and Safety Officer. Defaults to `admin`; change it. |
+| `SAFEX_ADMIN_PASSCODE` | `openssl rand -base64 24` | The password. 12 characters minimum or the console stays locked. |
+| `SAFEX_ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET` | `openssl rand -base64 32` | Signs the session cookie. Optional — derived from the password when unset, which means rotating the password logs everyone out. |
+| `SAFEX_ADMIN_PII_ENABLED` | `false` to start | Releases mobile, address and blood group to signed-in officers. Blood group is health data; turn it on deliberately, not by default. |
+| `SAFEX_EMPLOYEE_MASTER_CSV_URL` | your published sheet CSV link | See section 4. |
+| `SAFEX_EMPLOYEE_MASTER_SITE_ID` | `wbd` | |
+| `SAFEX_EMPLOYEE_MASTER_SITE_NAME` | `West Bokaro` | |
+| `SAFEX_EMPLOYEE_MASTER_SITE_CODE` | `WBD` | Shown as the badge beside the site name. |
+| `SAFEX_EMPLOYEE_MASTER_SITE_REGION` | `Ramgarh, Jharkhand` | |
+| `SAFEX_EMPLOYEE_MASTER_TTL_SECONDS` | `600` | How long a loaded roster is cached in server memory. |
+
+Never prefix any of these with `NEXT_PUBLIC_`. That prefix ships the value to every
+browser, which for the password means publishing it.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Real employee data
+
+Two ways in. Both are just environment variables — no code change.
+
+### Option A — the published Google Sheet (fastest)
+
+In Google Sheets: **File → Share → Publish to web → CSV**. Copy the generated link and set
+it as `SAFEX_EMPLOYEE_MASTER_CSV_URL` in Vercel.
+
+The URL is deliberately **not** committed to this repository. It is unauthenticated and
+world-readable, so anyone who finds it can download the whole roster — names, mobile
+numbers, addresses. Keeping it out of a public repo is the difference between "technically
+public" and "indexed".
+
+The parser already handles the quirks in the current sheet: leading spaces and embedded
+newlines in names and pass numbers, the `Siler`/`Siver`/`Sillver` misspellings of Silver,
+11-digit mobiles, and the skeleton rows that only have an ID and a name. See
+`docs/employee-master.md`.
+
+Verify after deploy:
+
+```bash
+curl -s https://YOUR-APP.vercel.app/api/bootstrap
+# expect: "directory":"sheet" and a real employeeCount
+```
+
+### Option B — Supabase (durable)
+
+**One file, one paste.** Open the Supabase SQL editor, paste `supabase/setup.sql`, Run.
+It is generated from the migrations in the only order that works and is safe to re-run:
+
+```bash
+npm run supabase:setup      # regenerate it after editing any migration
+```
+
+The last section is a verification suite. It changes nothing and prints one row per
+check. **If any row is not PASS, stop.** The checks that fail loudest are the ones that
+would otherwise leak one tenant's employees to another.
+
+Then set these in Vercel (and in `.env.local` for a local import):
+
+```
+NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=        # Project Settings → API → Project URL
+NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=   # same page, anon public key
+SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=       # same page, service_role — server only, never public
+SAFEX_TENANT_SLUG=emveess
+```
+
+`SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` bypasses every row-level security policy. It belongs in
+Vercel environment variables and nowhere else — not in the repo, not in chat, not in a
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable.
+
+Finally load the roster:
+
+```bash
+npm run employees:dry-run                 # parses and reports, writes nothing
+npm run employees:import -- --ensure-site # creates the West Bokaro site row, then upserts
+```
+
+`--ensure-site` is needed the first time because `employees.site_id` has a composite
+foreign key to `sites(id, tenant_id)` — the site row must exist before any employee can
+point at it. The upsert key is `(tenant_id, employee_no)`, so re-running updates in place
+and never duplicates.
+
+Supabase takes priority over the sheet automatically once it has rows, so you can move
+from Option A to Option B without touching the app.
+
+### What the real roster actually contains
+
+The importer was run in dry-run against the live sheet. **193 usable rows, 0 skipped**,
+and 18 issues worth knowing about before you rely on OTP sign-in:
+
+| Issue | Rows | Why it matters |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Shared mobile `+9193*****27` | EMP037, EMP191 | |
+| Shared mobile `+9162*****55` | EMP048, EMP139 | Same person twice — same name, same safety pass `WW0819173794` |
+| Shared mobile `+9170*****93` | EMP069, EMP083 | |
+| No usable mobile | 9 employees | |
+
+A shared mobile means an OTP cannot identify one person, so **those employees cannot be
+signed in by OTP until the sheet is corrected**. The importer reports them rather than
+guessing. Nine more have no usable number at all — including EMP001 and EMP002, whose
+numbers are 11 digits (`91777785011`, `99399844078`): the leading 91 looks like a country
+code typed into a mobile field, and the parser refuses to guess which digits to drop.
+
+Fix those in the sheet and re-run the import; nothing else needs changing.
+
+## 5. Before real users
+
+Still true today, and worth reading before you hand out the URL:
+
+- **The admin login is one shared account.** It has no per-user identity and no audit
+  trail, so a record of *who* opened a profile does not exist. It is the smallest thing
+  that is honestly safe in front of real personal data, not the right long-term answer.
+  Replace it with Supabase Auth plus a `staff_memberships` role check.
+- **Employee OTP sign-in is not connected.** The employee tab sends nothing and verifies
+  nothing. Wire real verification and SMS delivery before telling workers to use it.
+- **Report sync stays disabled** until schema, RLS, storage and secrets are verified in a
+  staging project.
+- **`SAFEX_ADMIN_PII_ENABLED=false` until you need it.** A home address can put someone at
+  risk and a blood group is health data.
+- **Rotate `SAFEX_ADMIN_PASSCODE`** whenever someone who knew it leaves.
